00:02:03.072 --> 00:02:04.530
MICHAEL WINDSOR:
So I want to start

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.738
by talking a bit about how
we came to this place, how

00:02:06.738 --> 00:02:08.490
we came to develop App Maker.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.071
And it starts really
with you, the customers.

00:02:12.071 --> 00:02:14.070
When we started having
conversation when I first

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.540
started at Google, talking
to customers about what

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.710
they were trying to do.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:17.700
As they would move
towards Google

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.080
they had all these
different problems

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.079
that they had previously
solved in various ways.

00:02:21.079 --> 00:02:23.190
Some good, some bad.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.440
And they wanted a way to
build these solutions running

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.110
on top of G Suite.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.540
And these are problems
that are not well met

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:31.980
by the major SaaS products.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.480
If you think about
whether it's just

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.074
G Suite, or Salesforce,
or Net Suite,

00:02:36.074 --> 00:02:37.990
or Marketo, all these
different things, right?

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:38.948
These are great things.

00:02:38.948 --> 00:02:40.217
They solve things very well.

00:02:40.217 --> 00:02:42.300
But they tend to sort of
normalize and commoditize

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.160
the problem domain
that you're working in.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.020
And you have less flexibility.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.194
And so if you think about
the main silos of software

00:02:48.194 --> 00:02:49.860
you have your line
of business software.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.469
That's how we make the donuts.

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:53.760
Operational software which
is how we pay for the donuts

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.690
or get them paid.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.850
And then productivity.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.560
The gaps in between
those spaces are

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.166
where businesses
try to automate,

00:03:00.166 --> 00:03:02.040
try to connect things,
and make it work well,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.960
whether it's order
fulfillment, or event planning.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.870
Whether it's, you
know, approvals

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:11.460
for various internal business
work processes, or scheduling,

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.400
you know, like
planning helpers where

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.233
I need to have a queue
of people can come in

00:03:15.233 --> 00:03:16.537
and book office hours.

00:03:16.537 --> 00:03:18.120
These are things
that today are solved

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.610
by horribly manual
processes involving

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.160
lots of emails, documents.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.730
We've all seen the
spreadsheet from hell, right?

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:28.650
Every business has somebody
who's basically unfireable

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.644
because they manage
that spreadsheet that

00:03:30.644 --> 00:03:32.810
is used for some incredibly
important crazy process.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.643
And everyone's here is
like, chuckling like,

00:03:34.643 --> 00:03:36.750
oh, yeah, I know that guy.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.806
And you know, when we see
people trying to solve this

00:03:39.806 --> 00:03:42.180
sometimes they go off and they
use various SaaS solutions

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:42.679
out there.

00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:43.620
They self serve.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.060
And that creates
admin headaches.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.611
They've got new silos of data,
new clouds of compliance.

00:03:47.611 --> 00:03:49.110
So admins are very
worried about all

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.450
these unauthorized
solutions coming in there.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.700
So this is the context in
which we came, and we really

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.241
want to start solving
those problems.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.640
GEVA RECHAV: I want to
help set the picture here

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.380
with one example.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.020
A few years ago at
Google, our recruiters

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.440
from the HR department reached
out to the IT department.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.480
We called this the corp eng.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.910
And they were facing a problem
of really wildly increasing

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.500
volume of candidates.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.169
We were starting to see
thousands of candidates

00:04:19.169 --> 00:04:21.000
at Google every month.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.620
Each one of those
was interviewed

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.090
by at least one interviewer.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.480
So we were getting a lot
of textual feedbacks being

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.740
piled up.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.600
And it was taking a long time.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.740
And it was really difficult
to assess these feedbacks.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.710
So we were looking into some
kind of manageable solution

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.440
that would also let the
recruiters calibrate

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.500
the different candidates
across different skill sets,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:48.440
and be able to get back to the
candidates in a timely manner.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.080
So we looked into
how we can build

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:55.840
an app that will do this
quickly and efficiently.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.130
In addition to this I ask
from the HR department

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.590
there were different areas,
different parts of Google

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.950
that had their own requests
and special processes

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.440
that they wanted to
tackle in a good way.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.140
So they were reaching
out to the IT department

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:14.640
continuously with more and
more app building requests.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.240
And the IT department
was also looking

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.550
to automate their own
internal processes,

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:24.600
as I believe many of you
look to automate processes

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:29.070
like dashboards to track a lot
of projects that are ongoing.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:32.190
So the IT department started
building business apps

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.890
to deal with their own workflow.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:38.940
We realized that we need a good
solution for low-code business

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.480
app development at Google
so that we could actually

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.830
make our software engineers very
efficient doing these custom

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:50.940
apps and spending the majority
of their time developing

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.220
the hard technical
problems that we have.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.400
And also, at the same
time, to empower a lot

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.620
of other different
people in the company

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:02.310
that have some level
of coding affinity

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:06.930
or like to actually
go ahead and develop

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:10.552
business apps on their own were
getting very little assistance.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.740
So this was really
taking off very well.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:20.950
And we were starting to see
a lot of internal apps being

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:24.220
built. And there were
lots of areas that were

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:27.100
getting addressed by App Maker.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.050
As you can see, in
the last three years

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.960
we've got to almost 400 apps.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.830
And they have currently 100s,
and some of them even thousands

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.537
of Googlers using
them, sometimes daily.

00:06:38.537 --> 00:06:40.120
A lot of the people
inside the company

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.078
are not even always aware
that they're actually

00:06:42.078 --> 00:06:44.440
using an app that was
built with app maker, which

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.180
is a great success.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.520
And the majority
of these apps was

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.560
built by non software engineers,
or with little software

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:54.981
engineering help.

00:06:54.981 --> 00:06:56.980
MICHAEL WINDSOR: And
people outside of corp eng.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:59.590
So cop eng was not tasked
with taking these things on.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.423
Other teams said, we're
going to build this.

00:07:01.423 --> 00:07:04.420
And corp eng said, that's OK
by us, which was a huge win.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.010
GEVA RECHAV: And
so the major point

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:08.140
that we want you
to take away here

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:12.400
is that we were seeing a
very impressive transition

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.330
and lowering of the bar for
building custom business apps

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:20.140
by moving the
development of these apps

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.330
from software engineers
in the IT department

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.222
solely to also seeing
them, you know,

00:07:25.222 --> 00:07:28.840
being built by a lot of other
different people outside of IT.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.460
MICHAEL WINDSOR:
So let's talk a bit

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.472
about why this is such
a point of pain, right?

00:07:35.472 --> 00:07:36.930
Why is it hard to
build these apps?

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.150
Why is IT not prioritizing
these applications?

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.570
And how can we do this better?

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:42.420
So earlier on I talked
about how, you know,

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.250
it's line of business,
operational, and productivity.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.430
Well, if you're a
business planner, when

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.292
you think about your
IT spend and how you're

00:07:49.292 --> 00:07:50.750
going to prioritize
your resources,

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:52.166
it's on the things
that absolutely

00:07:52.166 --> 00:07:53.190
matter to the business.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:55.464
Line of business first,
operation second,

00:07:55.464 --> 00:07:57.630
and productivity, and
everything else, and the gaps,

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:58.350
well, they come second.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:07:59.880
If there's a way for
humans to solve a problem

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.460
you're going to prioritize
expensive software development

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:03.660
elsewhere.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.550
And that is aggravated,
or exacerbated,

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.710
by the fact that the
stakeholders are coming

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:09.900
to you with problems
that they understand

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:10.951
from their perspective.

00:08:10.951 --> 00:08:12.450
But they're not
software developers.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:13.710
They're not product managers.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.585
So their ability to articulate a
plan about what we should build

00:08:16.585 --> 00:08:17.960
is just not their
problem domain.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:18.927
It's not what they do.

00:08:18.927 --> 00:08:20.010
They are sales specialist.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:20.990
They are HR specialists.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:22.281
They're all these other things.

00:08:22.281 --> 00:08:23.610
They're not product managers.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.307
So everybody knows in IT knows
when somebody from accounting

00:08:26.307 --> 00:08:28.890
comes to me and says, I got this
really important thing I need

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:31.630
you to build, they have no
idea what they're talking about

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:33.750
and you dread actually
trying to build it, right?

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.765
And that's a big problem.

00:08:35.765 --> 00:08:37.140
And then it's just
the basic fact

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.990
of building an application
is not a trivial exercise.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:40.900
We know how to do it.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:42.720
There's a well-defined
pattern we use.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.364
Different frameworks, different
app systems, you know,

00:08:45.364 --> 00:08:47.280
hosting environments,
and all of these things.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.071
But there's a lot of
work to stand that up.

00:08:49.071 --> 00:08:51.660
And then of course, keeping
it up, maintaining the code,

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:54.630
and operationalizing it, keeping
it running on an ongoing basis,

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:55.659
is non-trivial.

00:08:55.659 --> 00:08:57.450
So these are all things
that get in the way

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:58.450
of trying to do that.

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:01.080
And then, finally, you've got
your policy and admin people

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:04.110
telling you apps have to
conform to certain basic things.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.346
We don't want rogue apps, rogue
IT, rogue data everywhere.

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:08.970
So this is just a
big problem we really

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:10.510
need to take on
and solve, in terms

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:13.010
of how we build these apps.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.214
So we're really
excited about being

00:09:15.214 --> 00:09:16.880
able to bring App
Maker into this space,

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:18.770
because App Maker
is part of G Suite.

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:22.130
It means every G Suite
for Business customer

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:24.140
has access to App Maker.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:25.700
These applications run on--

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:26.705
and this was a
critical requirement,

00:09:26.705 --> 00:09:27.680
our customers were
very clear-- they're

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:29.242
run on G Suite infrastructure.

00:09:29.242 --> 00:09:30.950
So they leverage all
of the security, all

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:32.750
of the compliance, all
of the scalability,

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:35.060
all the operational
support, SRE,

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:39.701
as we call it, Site Reliability,
all of that is built in.

00:09:39.701 --> 00:09:41.450
You have as many
applications as you want.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:43.252
The pricing of
the application is

00:09:43.252 --> 00:09:44.960
tied into the G Suite
for Business thing.

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:46.310
It's paid for.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:46.820
All right?

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.650
So it scales to the size
of your organization.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.733
But you can have as many
applications as you want.

00:09:50.733 --> 00:09:52.858
You can have as many
databases, as you want, right?

00:09:52.858 --> 00:09:54.649
You don't have to worry
about that problem.

00:09:54.649 --> 00:09:57.050
So again, from an admin,
and compliance, and policy,

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.285
point of view, the cost
is built in to your G

00:09:59.285 --> 00:10:00.410
Suite for Business license.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.120
It scales to organization.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.990
And the whole goal is to extend,
to enhance your productivity

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:09.200
suites, whether it's G Suite
apps, or third party apps that

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.930
are part of your portfolio
to integrate them

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:14.930
and to create a more
solution oriented space

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.820
for your business users.

