00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.690
solutions internally at Google.

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.430
So I'm happy to
at least give you,

00:10:57.430 --> 00:10:59.760
hopefully, a quick demo of
what App Maker looks like.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.280
So let me just switch over into
how to build an application.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.140
So what I'm going
to do is just create

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.160
an application based
off of a spreadsheet

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:10.800
in just a matter of minutes.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:12.390
So in this case,
what we're looking at

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:14.400
is the App Maker environment.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.200
And I'm going to create
a new data model.

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:19.420
Also, I should point out this
is based off of a template.

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:22.080
So if you ever want to get
started with App Maker,

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.450
you actually have a whole
series of different template

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:25.020
applications.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.289
Some of them are
quite sophisticated.

00:11:27.289 --> 00:11:29.580
But in this case, I just use
like a simple starter app,

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:32.479
which gives us like the
menu, header, and so forth.

00:11:32.479 --> 00:11:34.270
So that's what we're
looking at right here.

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:36.550
So let's create a
backend data model.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:38.550
So essentially this is
like the backend database

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:39.930
for our application.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:41.940
It's going to utilize
Google Drive Table, which

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:44.880
is a new database that
you get for free whenever

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:46.230
you build in App Maker.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.910
And I'm going to create a model.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.920
In this case it's like
a series of stores,

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.650
like a global registry of stores
that we need to set up a way

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:55.020
to do on site inspections.

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:58.070
And so in this case I'm going to
first click on Import the field

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:01.770
where I can go ahead and base
the App Maker data model off

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:02.845
of this spreadsheet.

00:12:02.845 --> 00:12:04.720
So I happen to have my
spreadsheet accessible

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:06.764
in the Google Drive
Picker window.

00:12:06.764 --> 00:12:08.430
And I once I select
it, it will actually

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:10.230
scan a little bit of the
data so I can visually

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:11.938
confirm that, yep,
that's the data that I

00:12:11.938 --> 00:12:13.797
want to base my new model of.

00:12:13.797 --> 00:12:15.630
It will then go through
it and, essentially,

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:18.210
guess at what are the
data types for my fields.

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:20.790
And I'll click Create.

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:22.427
And now we have
a new data model.

00:12:22.427 --> 00:12:24.760
Now, if I wanted to, I could
go in and further customize

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.940
the fields, like adding
validation and such.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:29.190
And while I'm at it I'm going
to go ahead and actually

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:30.490
import the data as well.

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:33.120
So let's just go ahead
and select the store.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:35.180
And since I have the
global store sheet handy

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:37.620
it'll keep that ready to go.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:39.210
And that will start
the connection now

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:42.360
to the actual spreadsheet and
start pulling that data over.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:44.450
And I can even return
back to the main page.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:47.240
And while that import is
done-- oh there we go.

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:48.360
It's already finished.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.597
Now I can switch over and
start building the UI.

00:12:50.597 --> 00:12:52.680
Now, the first thing that
you typically want to do

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.860
is set up like a default
data source for your UI.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.350
So in this case, I'm just
going to point it to the stores

00:12:58.350 --> 00:12:59.950
directly.

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:02.340
So that makes data
binding a lot easier.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:06.150
So I can start to drag and drop
widgets, which in this case

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:07.670
I just drag a form.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:10.650
And so the idea being that I
can build forms, tables, really

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:14.430
any UI construct very easily
by dragging and dropping

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:14.961
the widgets.

00:13:14.961 --> 00:13:16.710
In this case it's like
a composite widget.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:18.926
It's going to build me
an entire insert form.

00:13:18.926 --> 00:13:20.800
And so for this one I
want to keep it simple.

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:22.320
I just want a couple of fields.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:24.190
I want the location.

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:25.790
And I want the inspection date.

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:27.840
And we'll leave it at as that.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:28.860
I'll click Finish.

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:29.590
So there we go.

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:31.050
We've got a form that
got generated for me.

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:32.424
If I want to
customize it further

00:13:32.424 --> 00:13:34.440
I could take out some
fields or whatever.

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:38.910
And now I'm going to
drag and drop a table.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:41.280
And the table is going
to essentially iterate

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:42.840
through all the records.

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:45.920
And for this one I'm just
going to select location.

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:46.890
I'll add a rating.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:48.690
So like when you do
a site inspection

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:50.120
you can put that data in there.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:51.120
And of course, the date.

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:54.060
And for this one I'm going
to have editable fields.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:55.740
And I click Next.

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:57.120
And Finish that off.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:00.330
So now we have a design
time view of our table.

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.430
Let me just kind
of size it a bit.

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:05.080
So as you can see with the
App Maker visual environment,

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:08.140
it's quite easy to
customize the UI as needed.

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.970
And I'm going to
add another widget.

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:13.140
So I just do like a quick
search on the palette.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:16.320
And we happen to have
a Google map widget.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:19.150
I'll just drop
that onto the page.

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:21.540
In this case, I'm just
going to kind of resize it

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:27.840
a bit so that it will stretch
over to the size of the top two

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:29.610
forms.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.970
And this is where it gets,
fun because I can data

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:35.280
bind the widget over here
in the property editor.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:37.590
And I pop it open
into binding mode.

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:38.970
And I just set the location.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.784
So now, whenever I'm pointing
to a record it will show that.

00:14:41.784 --> 00:14:43.200
Now, there's another
customization

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:44.154
that I'm going to do.

00:14:44.154 --> 00:14:45.570
Right now I just
have a text field

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:47.100
that got generated for me.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:51.870
But I'm going to spice it up a
bit with a star rating widget.

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:54.360
So I drag and drop the
star rating widget,

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.860
stick it right
next to the rating,

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:58.590
and you can blow
away the rating.

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:01.340
And then you can guess what
my next step is, right?

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:05.070
I need to set the value to
a data bound value coming

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:06.360
from my backend database.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:08.050
And I set it to rating.

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:09.810
And so that's
basically it for now.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:11.520
I'm going to go ahead
and click Preview.

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:12.936
So in this sense,
what it's doing,

00:15:12.936 --> 00:15:15.450
it's taking what I've done,
what I've designed visually,

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.340
compiling that
into an App Script,

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:19.660
and sent over to the
App Script run time.

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:21.840
So now we are
looking at this data

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:24.060
right here where I can
click through, and maybe

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:27.520
sort the data, or even directly
change some of the data.

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:29.520
And of course if I wanted
to add some data here,

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:32.085
like maybe our Paris location.

00:15:34.985 --> 00:15:38.724
And maybe select
a particular date.

00:15:38.724 --> 00:15:40.140
I love the
accessibility features.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:42.870
I can do all that very easily.

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:44.440
And so now I have a new record.

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.950
So we have a store in
Paris that we go inspect.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:49.710
All right so let's go back
and add a little bit more

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:51.460
functionality to
the application.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:54.840
So in this case, I'm going to
add a little bit of scripting.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:57.570
And the idea being that
whenever I insert a new record

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:00.656
I might want to send an email
to the person that's actually,

00:16:00.656 --> 00:16:02.030
you know, inserting
or requesting

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:03.330
a new store inspection.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:04.630
And so how can I do that?

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:08.136
Well, I happen to have a
little bit of code here.

00:16:08.136 --> 00:16:10.260
which I'll go through it
a little bit more closely.

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.450
But basically this
is a bit of code

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:14.790
that's just using
App Script directly

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:19.570
to then send an email to
the person who's doing that.

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:21.690
So now all I have to do
is create a new script.

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:24.540
So we have a nice script
generation wizard.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:26.867
And it will even generate
some sample code as well.

00:16:26.867 --> 00:16:29.200
And so this script is going
to be running on the server.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:30.660
So it's running App Script.

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:32.324
So I click Create.

00:16:32.324 --> 00:16:33.990
And there's a little
bit of example code

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:35.120
that you can learn from.

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:38.080
In this case I just paste that
new function that I created.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:41.310
And this is a function
called Notify.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:43.950
And I want this to be
called whenever I create

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:45.280
a new record in my database.

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:49.980
So I can go back to my data
model, click on Events,

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.980
and then on the On
Create Event I could just

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:55.170
call the function directly.

00:16:55.170 --> 00:17:01.080
And here I can specify
the actual record,

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:04.619
which contains the location as
well as the inspection date.

00:17:04.619 --> 00:17:05.950
Those are just data objects.

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:10.329
So that's going to fire off that
whenever I create a new record.

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:13.079
So let's go ahead and
hit preview again.

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:15.300
Also I should check
my email here.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.880
So there's no e-mails
in the queue, for now.

00:17:20.808 --> 00:17:23.441
I don't want to
turn off my WiFi.

00:17:23.441 --> 00:17:23.940
All right.

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:25.720
So let's create
a new inspection.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:27.329
Michael, Geva, any location?

00:17:27.329 --> 00:17:28.410
GEVA RECHAV: We're
going to Helsinki.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:28.860
CHRISTIAN SCHALK: Helsinki?

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:29.140
OK.

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:29.848
GEVA RECHAV: Yup.

00:17:29.848 --> 00:17:33.022
CHRISTIAN SCHALK:
Helsinki it is.

00:17:33.022 --> 00:17:34.980
GEVA RECHAV: I hear it's
really nice right now.

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:38.490
CHRISTIAN SCHALK: Helsinki
and what date do we want.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:39.810
Some time--

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:41.185
GEVA RECHAV: I'd
like to go there

00:17:41.185 --> 00:17:43.305
in the cold, dark
times of winter.

00:17:43.305 --> 00:17:44.930
CHRISTIAN SCHALK:
How does June, sound?

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:46.370
OK.

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:46.870
Yeah.

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:47.210
All right.

00:17:47.210 --> 00:17:47.520
There it goes.

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:48.250
So we're all set.

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:51.190
Now, let's go check our email
and see if we got a message.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:52.710
Surprise, Surprise.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:54.450
I have an inspection alert.

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:55.261
It says, greetings.

00:17:55.261 --> 00:17:57.510
Your store at the Helsinki
location will be inspected.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:58.322
Blah, blah, blah.

00:17:58.322 --> 00:17:59.280
So that's basically it.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:00.570
It hopefully gives
you an idea of how

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:02.650
you can work with App Maker
starting with a spreadsheet.

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:04.483
Drag and drop some
widgets, put a little bit

00:18:04.483 --> 00:18:06.480
of coding in there, and
we're just literally

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:07.450
scratching the surface.

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:10.080
So if you have a chance,
please come to our demo grounds

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:11.220
on the third floor
by [? Schoening. ?]

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.330
We'll show you a whole
bunch more stuff as well.

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:15.226
MICHAEL WINDSOR: So I want
to take a moment here.

00:18:15.226 --> 00:18:15.726
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:15.726 --> 00:18:18.154
That's awesome.

00:18:18.154 --> 00:18:21.090
Very nice.

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:22.274
And I wasn't timing it.

00:18:22.274 --> 00:18:22.940
I really should.

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:26.550
Like that was five, maybe
eight minutes to build an app.

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:28.522
Now, that's not an
app that, you know,

00:18:28.522 --> 00:18:30.480
is everything everybody
could possibly imagine.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:32.820
But it's an app that
can be used right away.

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:35.460
And it's like literally a
personification of Agile

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:37.590
where this is what you
asked me for, show me

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:39.746
what you need next, show
me what you used, right?

00:18:39.746 --> 00:18:41.370
You can put in
analytics into your app.

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:42.120
You can start gathering data.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:44.078
You can start knowing
what your customers need.

00:18:44.078 --> 00:18:46.380
And the investment of time
there was trivial, right?

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.750
And you can easily imagine
that person who knows nothing

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:51.000
about product development
sitting down next to someone

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:54.080
like Chris and saying, I
need something to do this.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:55.830
And it starts with a
spreadsheet and turns

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:57.990
into a usable application
where now it's

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.685
managed, auditable,
trackable, everything,

00:19:00.685 --> 00:19:02.810
and you can start integrating
into other workflows.

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:03.960
And then all of
the things that you

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:05.820
want to start having happening
are no longer like, oh,

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:07.569
god I'm back in that
spreadsheet from hell

00:19:07.569 --> 00:19:09.720
trying to write a macro.

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:12.154
So these are just some
of the other customers

00:19:12.154 --> 00:19:14.320
that we've worked with early
on in the stage of sort

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:15.360
of building out App Maker.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.068
They've been our
partners for a long time

00:19:17.068 --> 00:19:19.050
in helping us flesh
out the product,

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:20.662
build real life use cases.

00:19:20.662 --> 00:19:21.870
I just want to call them out.

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:23.580
In particular, State of
Colorado, Sanmina, me

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:24.750
and the State of Wyoming.

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:25.550
Is Flint here?

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:27.660
Flint, are you here?

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:28.620
I can't see him.

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:30.990
You're all dark bodies in a
gray background right now.

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:34.212
But Flint is actually was the
CIO of the State of Wyoming.

00:19:34.212 --> 00:19:35.670
They got in and
got in really early

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:36.510
and did some great things.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:38.926
And he's actually loved it so
much he now works at Google.

00:19:38.926 --> 00:19:41.150
So that worked out
really well for us.

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:42.150
No, it doesn't work out.

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:43.775
If you all build App
Maker applications

00:19:43.775 --> 00:19:45.540
I can't hire every
single one of you.

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:46.982
I'm not allowed to do that.

00:19:46.982 --> 00:19:49.440
So I want to talk a little
about one of our other customers

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.870
who've also been a
tremendous partner with us.

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.630
They got involved
relatively early, as well.

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:56.700
Ocado is an online
retailer in the UK.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:58.240
They have a lot of employees.

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:00.819
They have a lot of
technology investment.

00:20:00.819 --> 00:20:02.610
There's a big engineering
department there.

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:04.920
But like I said, those
engineers are very focused

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:06.954
on their core line of
business requirements.

00:20:06.954 --> 00:20:08.370
As the business
is growing they're

00:20:08.370 --> 00:20:10.190
solving business problems.

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:12.450
But they have a lot of people
doing things, including

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:15.510
training, going to
conferences like this,

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:17.670
taking on training
activities and so forth.

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:21.780
And this was literally
just an entire mess

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:25.110
of emails, manual processes,
multiple spreadsheets.

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:26.880
I'm sure paper was
involved at some point.

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:28.609
It was an incredibly
taxing process.

00:20:28.609 --> 00:20:29.400
It was inefficient.

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:31.100
Nobody knew what was going on.

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:33.600
If you wanted to find out what
conferences had been approved

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:35.649
to go to or were
standard process

00:20:35.649 --> 00:20:36.690
there was no way to know.

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:39.450
You just had to know
