the Google services,

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:08.050
those are familiar,
and like that.

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:13.090
Drive Tables is the built in
storage module for App Maker.

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:15.700
It stores data in its
own private data store.

00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:17.650
It's highly performant.

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:19.452
It's not meant to scale
to giant data sets.

00:30:19.452 --> 00:30:21.160
So please don't think
this is a cheap way

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:23.830
to get BigQuery going
in your little world.

00:30:23.830 --> 00:30:25.790
And we'll talk about
BigQuery in a second.

00:30:25.790 --> 00:30:28.634
But it is exactly what you need
for 90% of your applications.

00:30:28.634 --> 00:30:30.550
And when you feel like
your needs have changed

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:32.508
or you want to interop
with other systems data,

00:30:32.508 --> 00:30:33.670
as well, right?

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:35.507
You can switch to
using Cloud SQL.

00:30:35.507 --> 00:30:37.090
And then you'll also
be able to switch

00:30:37.090 --> 00:30:39.381
to postgreSQL, or MySQL,
and then other data sources,

00:30:39.381 --> 00:30:39.880
as well.

00:30:39.880 --> 00:30:41.780
So that's a list of
expanding data connectors

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:43.030
that we're going to build out.

00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:44.390
And then those same
data connector things

00:30:44.390 --> 00:30:46.630
will also work for talking to
SaaS services that are not,

00:30:46.630 --> 00:30:48.910
strictly speaking, a database,
but maybe like Salesforce,

00:30:48.910 --> 00:30:51.550
or something like that where you
want to get to enterprise data

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:54.077
and build the data
into your application.

00:30:54.077 --> 00:30:56.160
Before you-- no, I was
going to do something else.

00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:56.660
Nope.

00:30:56.660 --> 00:30:58.820
I've already forgot
what I was going to do.

00:30:58.820 --> 00:31:01.309
So another brief call
out to State of Wyoming,

00:31:01.309 --> 00:31:03.600
because it really illustrates
how things came together.

00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:06.307
So the State of Wyoming
has a great IT department.

00:31:06.307 --> 00:31:08.640
They've been building out
things on G Suite for a while.

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:10.620
They've done App Script,
and various things.

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:13.980
They have a team that is
responsible for, essentially,

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:17.437
security of their
software environment.

00:31:17.437 --> 00:31:19.020
And they have a set
of tools that they

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:21.840
use to track and detect,
you know, phishing

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:23.250
attacks, and various things.

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:24.960
They leverage G Suite's
built in services

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:26.970
but they have a bunch of
other products as well.

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:28.636
And they wanted to
kind of pull together

00:31:28.636 --> 00:31:30.870
a 360 dashboard of all the
things that could happen.

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:32.760
This has been a
longstanding wish of theirs

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:34.860
but they could never
really pull it together.

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:37.530
Again, with App Maker they sat
down and said, we can do this.

00:31:37.530 --> 00:31:38.640
And I want to talk
about this picture

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:40.230
here because this
is quite literally--

00:31:40.230 --> 00:31:42.900
the guy with the hat,
he's the engineer, right?

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:44.790
And the other people
there are the people

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:46.530
who are sitting there thinking
about the business problem.

00:31:46.530 --> 00:31:47.790
They are the stakeholders here.

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:48.630
They are not engineers.

00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:49.800
They're software developers.

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:51.550
And this picture is
obviously a little bit

00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:54.060
staged but it is actually
how things happened.

00:31:54.060 --> 00:31:56.652
They sat down and said, what we
want is this information here.

00:31:56.652 --> 00:31:58.110
We want to track
these things here.

00:31:58.110 --> 00:32:00.110
We're going to pull these
things together there.

00:32:00.110 --> 00:32:02.170
And the engineer was able
to do this on the fly.

00:32:02.170 --> 00:32:04.295
And in a matter of days
they had something working.

00:32:04.295 --> 00:32:06.336
In a matter of weeks they
had something deployed.

00:32:06.336 --> 00:32:08.490
And they are now actively
tracking and building out

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:10.890
this application as a way
to track their security

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:13.452
events across the
organization, how to respond,

00:32:13.452 --> 00:32:14.910
how to have a
workflow around them,

00:32:14.910 --> 00:32:16.170
so when something
happens it's not just,

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:17.310
hey, something seems bad.

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:19.680
There's a whole process
that kicks off around that.

00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:22.140
So really great story
for how you can build out

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:24.480
applications and
compress that cycle.

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:27.840
Where the stakeholders, the
engineering get to market,

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.990
get to usage,
happens much faster.

00:32:29.990 --> 00:32:31.740
So you learn what you
need to know faster.

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:33.330
And you spend less
risk, less time,

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:36.339
and less effort getting there.

00:32:36.339 --> 00:32:37.880
GEVA RECHAV: We want
all of you to be

00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:40.730
able to focus on
your business needs

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:45.230
and be able to spec what kind
of problems you need to solve.

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:48.020
And we want to
help you identify,

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:50.830
what are the best candidates
in your companies to actually

00:32:50.830 --> 00:32:52.670
build App Maker apps around.

00:32:52.670 --> 00:32:55.820
So we want to share some
of the areas where we've

00:32:55.820 --> 00:33:01.520
seen a lot of hairiness
and business workflows

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:07.940
that require you know,
better use and more

00:33:07.940 --> 00:33:09.860
productive workflows.

00:33:09.860 --> 00:33:12.110
So the first one, which
we've already mentioned here

00:33:12.110 --> 00:33:14.150
several times is sheets.

00:33:14.150 --> 00:33:16.610
Sheets, especially
very large ones.

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:20.000
Especially the ones that have
more than a handful of people

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:22.250
updating them on
the regular basis,

00:33:22.250 --> 00:33:24.800
usually hide some
kind of process

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:29.550
that's being done internally,
and hasn't been fleshed out

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:31.430
to the extreme.

00:33:31.430 --> 00:33:35.030
And so a lot of times
there are errors happening.

00:33:35.030 --> 00:33:37.700
Several times people are
updating the wrong cells.

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:40.850
They're taking decisions
based on information

00:33:40.850 --> 00:33:42.140
that's maybe not accurate.

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:47.240
And in general, you know, it's
not a very good experience

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:50.280
to start diving into a sheet and
look for the information that

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:51.660
is especially critical to you.

00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:57.620
So this is an area where taking
App Maker, spec'ing your UI,

00:33:57.620 --> 00:34:00.230
binding it to this data
source, to the sheet,

00:34:00.230 --> 00:34:02.390
and building the
process in this UI,

00:34:02.390 --> 00:34:06.220
could really change
things for the better.

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:10.090
Another area that we've seen
is around administrative tasks.

00:34:10.090 --> 00:34:13.780
And that relates to bulk
and recurring operations

00:34:13.780 --> 00:34:16.179
with other G Suite
core services.

00:34:16.179 --> 00:34:19.239
Especially Calendar and Gmail.

00:34:19.239 --> 00:34:22.780
If we look, for example, at the
store inspection application

00:34:22.780 --> 00:34:26.020
that we saw, this is something
that, without the application,

00:34:26.020 --> 00:34:29.800
requires scheduling tens
of events across the year,

00:34:29.800 --> 00:34:33.460
attaching documents to
those calendar invites.

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:36.130
There must be errors if
you want to update those,

00:34:36.130 --> 00:34:39.730
you again have to find
the right calendar entry,

00:34:39.730 --> 00:34:41.230
and update that one.

00:34:41.230 --> 00:34:43.945
Same way if you're sending
updates, or reporters,

00:34:43.945 --> 00:34:46.280
or if you're requesting
information from customers,

00:34:46.280 --> 00:34:47.679
vendors, partners.

00:34:47.679 --> 00:34:53.920
You're generating the same kind
of Gmail blasts every week,

00:34:53.920 --> 00:34:55.150
or 10 days, or months.

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:57.010
And this is another
area where you

00:34:57.010 --> 00:35:01.990
could automate and have a much
more fluid experience with App

00:35:01.990 --> 00:35:02.860
Maker.

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:05.500
The third area that we have
seen where people could benefit

00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:08.930
from building an app is
where you use BigQuery a lot,

00:35:08.930 --> 00:35:10.660
where you're calling APIs.

00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:14.710
You're already using
scripting in extensive ways

00:35:14.710 --> 00:35:18.640
so that means that you've
probably already tackled

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:22.390
some complex
workflows, but in order

00:35:22.390 --> 00:35:26.550
to maintain those workflows,
change them, make the UI nicer

00:35:26.550 --> 00:35:29.300
it would be much better to
use App Maker to tackle those.

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:30.010
MICHAEL WINDSOR: And I
want to talk a little

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:31.600
about Big Query for a second,
cause that was the thing I

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:32.290
forgot to talk about.

00:35:32.290 --> 00:35:34.495
We've actually seen an
increasingly common pattern.

00:35:34.495 --> 00:35:37.030
You know, BigQuery is not free.

00:35:37.030 --> 00:35:39.880
And people want to have various
ways into the BigQuery data.

00:35:39.880 --> 00:35:40.990
And what people are
starting to build

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:42.690
is App Maker applications
that are essentially

00:35:42.690 --> 00:35:44.860
this front end to your
BigQuery experience, where

00:35:44.860 --> 00:35:46.480
it helps the user
construct the query,

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:47.440
figure out what
they're trying to do,

00:35:47.440 --> 00:35:50.290
connect the various pieces
they want, runs the BigQuery,

00:35:50.290 --> 00:35:53.440
and then caches the results in
a local data store, which then

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:54.940
can be used for
multiple traversals,

00:35:54.940 --> 00:35:56.710
and sort of exploring,
and exports, and whatever.

00:35:56.710 --> 00:35:58.900
So you essentially use it
as a cache for the output

00:35:58.900 --> 00:36:00.070
of a BigQuery response.

00:36:00.070 --> 00:36:02.780
You might choose not to rerun
the query over and over again,

00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:04.030
because the user's just
sitting there poking around

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:04.960
at the data.

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:06.430
It's been tremendously
effective.

00:36:06.430 --> 00:36:07.805
It's a great
pattern to build on.

00:36:07.805 --> 00:36:11.820
We're going to start building
out samples in the same way.

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:12.320
Thanks.

